## THE SHAPE OF A DEFECT IN A CODEBASE THIS SMALL

Both tickets in this project are boundary defects in pure functions — a value at the edge of a
range is classified into the wrong bucket, or the last element of a sequence is not emitted. In
that shape the site that PRODUCES is the comparison, the operator, or the loop bound ITSELF,
because that expression is what brings the corrected value into existence. There is usually
exactly one such site, and it is one line.

That makes two mistakes especially cheap to fall into here, and both are fatal:

  1. PRESCRIBING THE TEST FILE AS THE FIX. `test/fares.test.ts` and `test/schedule.test.ts` are
     vitest specs. A test does not produce the value; at most it VERIFIES one. If a test file
     belongs in your answer at all, it is `"deliveryRole":"verifies"` with
     `"changeRequired":false` — and only when the existing spec will genuinely pass once `src/`
     is corrected. If the current spec ASSERTS the buggy behaviour, then it must be edited, and
     that is `changeRequired:true` — but it is still not the site that produces. Read the spec
     with `show` before you decide which of those two it is; do not guess from the filename.

  2. QUOTING THE OPERATOR YOU EXPECT INSTEAD OF THE ONE THAT IS THERE. `>=` and `>`, `<` and
     `<=`, `length` and `length - 1` are one character apart, and a `brokenLine` is checked
     character-for-character. Run `show <file> <start> <end>` on the actual region and COPY the
     line. A boundary bug is precisely the case where reconstructing the line from memory
     reproduces the correct code rather than the broken code, and the answer is rejected.

State the boundary in the language of values, not of intent: name the exact number, operator or
index involved (for example, which comparison excludes an age, or which bound stops one element
short), so the implementer changes one character and not a paragraph.

